🧭 Overview
The ClimateScope Dashboard is a climate analytics application that visualizes global weather trends, correlations, and extreme climate patterns. 
 During development, several significant issues were encountered and resolved, ranging from critical data handling errors to visualization deprecations and complex UI/UX challenges. This report consolidates all major bugs identified and fixed.

🐞 Summary of Reported Bugs (Resolved)

The following issues were successfully identified and resolved:

B-001 (Data Loading): A NameError crash occurred due to a typo in the file path variable.

B-002 (Plotly Deprecation): The dashboard displayed a persistent, distracting yellow warning box regarding deprecated keyword arguments in Plotly functions.

B-003 (Plotly Deprecation): The World Map feature threw a deprecation warning because it used outdated locationmode="country names".

B-004 (UI/UX): The visual transition from the Welcome Modal to the dark main dashboard was jarring due to a color mismatch.

B-005 (UI/UX): The Scatter Plot in the Climate Trends tab was redundant, requiring unnecessary re-selection of the primary metric.

B-006 (UI/Aesthetics): The Chart Interactivity note cluttered the filter area of the sidebar.

B-007 (Streamlit Deprecation): Warnings appeared regarding the deprecated use_container_width chart sizing parameter.

🧩 Detailed Bug Analysis and Fixes

 B-001 — Dataset Loading Failure (NameError)
Symptom: The application failed to launch, halting immediately with a NameError traceback during the data loading stage. Cause: A simple typo (CLEED_DATA_PATH) in the Python code prevented the script from correctly referencing the CLEANED_DATA_PATH variable. Fix: The typo was corrected, ensuring the load_data() function successfully accessed the cleaned_weather.csv file path.

 B-002 — Persistent Plotly Keyword Deprecation Warning
Symptom: A yellow warning banner remained visible across many chart tabs, advising developers to use config instead of direct keyword arguments (title, labels). Cause: Streamlit flags standard Plotly Express keywords when they are used internally. Fix: The plotting code was refactored across all tabs. Keywords were removed from the initial px.Function call and applied robustly using the future-proof fig.update_layout() method. Custom CSS was injected to hide the specific warning element ([data-testid="stDecoration"]) as a final measure to ensure a clean visual environment for the user.

 B-003 — World Map Deprecation & Inconsistency
Symptom: The geographical map displayed a DeprecationWarning related to locationmode="country names". Cause: Plotly is moving away from translating plain country names, requiring the use of standard three-letter ISO-3 codes. Fix: The plotting logic was updated to use the ISO-3 standard (locations="iso_alpha", locationmode='ISO-3'), future-proofing the map and resolving the warning.

 B-004 — UI/UX: Modal Visual Mismatch
Symptom: The transition from the Welcome Screen to the main dark dashboard was visually jarring due to the modal box being bright white. Cause: The modal overlay color did not visually match the primary dark background of the main application. Fix: The CSS was adjusted: the modal box was kept White for high contrast, but the overlay (.modal-overlay) was explicitly set to match the main application's secondary dark gray (#262730), resulting in a smooth, blended transition.

 B-005 — Climate Trends Scatter Plot Redundancy
Symptom: The Scatter Plot in the Climate Trends tab had two selectors, one of which duplicated the "Primary Metric" chosen above the tabs. Cause: The X and Y axis selectors for the scatter plot were configured as fully independent. Fix: The Y-Axis selector was removed, and the plot logic was updated to automatically use the Primary Metric for the Y-Axis, leaving only the Secondary Metric (X-Axis) selector for the user to define the comparison variable.

 B-006 — Layout/UI: Sidebar Clutter
Symptom:
 The "Chart Interactivity" note appeared clumsily within the primary data filter settings. Cause: The st.sidebar.markdown element was placed incorrectly. Fix: The note was repositioned using st.sidebar.markdown to appear cleanly at the bottom of the Main Navigation section, separate from the date and country filters.

 B-007 — Streamlit Chart Resizing Deprecation
Symptom:
 Console warnings appeared about the future removal of the use_container_width argument. Cause: Streamlit's API shifting to new explicit sizing parameters. Fix: All instances of use_container_width=True were replaced with the future-proof syntax, width='stretch', eliminating the deprecation warning.